1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,010
Most escrow demos show you money moving.

2
00:00:05,010 --> 00:00:07,520
This one starts with an agent refusing to

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:10,390
move it. A buyer just paid, and Coach

4
00:00:10,390 --> 00:00:11,280
said no.

5
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:16,290
Kajota Coach is a CFO for onchain

6
00:00:16,290 --> 00:00:18,620
escrow. It decides which payments are

7
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:21,240
safe to release, and KeeperHub signs the

8
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:22,120
transaction.

9
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:25,110
Coach never touches a private key. But the

10
00:00:25,110 --> 00:00:27,710
interesting part isn't when it

11
00:00:27,710 --> 00:00:28,600
says yes.

12
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:31,390
Money moved into escrow — and nothing

13
00:00:31,390 --> 00:00:33,960
else happened yet. The buyer hasn't

14
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:37,020
confirmed anything, so Coach holds it and

15
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:39,760
tells you exactly which rule failed.

16
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:43,750
Now the buyer confirms. Same six

17
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:47,440
rules, one signal different. Release.

18
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:51,200
KeeperHub signs it. Fifteen seconds, on Sepolia.

19
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:54,040
People ask where the AI is. Here's the

20
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:57,430
honest answer. The release decision is

21
00:00:57,430 --> 00:01:01,480
deterministic. Six rules, same inputs, same

22
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:03,240
verdict, every time.

23
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:06,210
Money shouldn't move on a sampled

24
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:08,990
token. The model does the job it's

25
00:01:08,990 --> 00:01:12,470
actually better at — reading what a buyer

26
00:01:12,470 --> 00:01:15,170
wrote and deciding if it's a real

27
00:01:15,170 --> 00:01:15,960
complaint.

28
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:20,240
No keyword list reads English. And it can

29
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:24,380
only ever block a release. It can never

30
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:25,240
cause one.

31
00:01:25,240 --> 00:01:28,380
While building this, we hit three field-name

32
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:31,470
traps in KeeperHub's own docs. One of them

33
00:01:31,470 --> 00:01:33,930
silently ignores the field you set and

34
00:01:33,930 --> 00:01:36,440
signs with a different wallet.

35
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:38,960
We filed the fix. KeeperHub merged it.

36
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:41,720
Then we built the tool that enforces it,

37
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:44,320
so Coach audits its own workflow before

38
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:45,560
it ever fires.

39
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:50,580
None of this needs a browser open. There's

40
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:55,690
a loop on the server reading Sepolia, running the same

41
00:01:55,690 --> 00:01:58,580
rules on a timer. It released that one on

42
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:01,080
its own. Nobody clicked anything.

43
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:04,230
Coach decides. KeeperHub ships. And

44
00:02:04,230 --> 00:02:07,310
every decision is one you can read, rule

45
00:02:07,310 --> 00:02:10,680
by rule, before the money arrives.
